WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.089
The Hulk is one of Marvel's most popular characters.

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:10.560
So it's no surprise the company turned to
the big green guy when it came time to launch

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.370
the Marvel Cinematic Universe in 2008.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:21.579
Hulk laid low for four years after his debut,
and was the breakout hit of The Avengers...so

00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:24.100
why haven't we seen more of the big green
guy?

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.439
Here are a few reasons why Marvel won't give
Hulk another movie.

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:31.929
A history of flops

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:38.219
Because the Hulk is such an iconic character,
the pressure was on in 2003 for Ang Lee's

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:42.539
Hulk film, which starred Eric Bana as Bruce
Banner.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:48.710
The movie wasn't terrible, but it wasn't great
either, earning lukewarm ratings across the

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:49.710
board.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.650
And it definitely wasn't good enough to warrant
a sequel.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:58.999
Fast forward five years, and the fledgeling
Marvel Studios was looking to take advantage

00:00:58.999 --> 00:01:05.059
of two of the major characters still under
their control to anchor new film franchises:

00:01:05.059 --> 00:01:07.170
Iron Man and the Hulk.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.720
Iron Man hit first, and thanks to the charisma
of Robert Downey, Jr., it hit big.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.700
The Incredible Hulk was the second film on
the slate, debuting a few months after Iron

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.210
Man, and it was… not as good.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:27.540
The film received decent reviews and was a
so-so performer at the box office, but compared

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.780
to the coolness and profitability of Iron
Man, Hulk just didn't hold up.

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.740
When it came time to choose which character
to pursue, Hulk got smashed.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.060
" Go to sleep, go to sleep, go to sleep."

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.240
A cancelled sequel

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.880
Marvel was already planning another Hulk film
pretty early on, but with the popularity of

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.479
Marvel's other characters, the project was
shelved.

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:59.590
Edward Norton was eventually axed and recast
with Mark Ruffalo in The Avengers.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:06.600
In a 2008 interview with GamesRadar, Ed Norton
spilled the beans on why The Incredible Hulk

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.060
felt unfinished, saying,

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.470
"We left a lot out on purpose.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.830
It's definitely intended as a chapter one.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:18.720
My attitude was, 'Let's start with a mystery
and slowly unpeel what the roots of it are,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.890
all the way through—like let's have revelations
even in the third act about what set this

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.019
whole thing in motion.'"

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:31.909
Unfortunately, those plans never materialized
for Norton, but a similar strategy has been

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:34.489
planned for Mark Ruffalo's Hulk.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:35.810
Ruffalo told Yahoo!

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.060
Movies that Hulk will be getting his due over
the next few years, saying,

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.800
"We've worked a really interesting arc into
Thor 3, Avengers 3 and 4 for Banner that I

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.769
think will—when it's all added up—will
feel like a Hulk movie, a standalone movie."

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:53.159
A full schedule

00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:59.890
Between sequels and originals, Marvel Studios
has cranked out a mind-boggling 14 movies

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:03.549
over the last eight years, but they're still
just getting started.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:10.370
They already have at least six movies currently
shooting or at some stage in production, including

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.110
Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2, Black Panther,
and Thor: Ragnarok.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:20.080
Even if Marvel wanted to make a new Hulk film,
it would be at least four years before they

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.310
could even fit it in the schedule.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.980
Considering just about every Marvel movie
is a hit, and they have to keep the fun train

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:32.319
rolling by introducing new characters, it's
hard to make space for a guy who already had

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:35.450
two shots at solo glory.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.150
Hulk isn't cheap

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:42.660
Making a movie where the main character can
turn into a giant green monster isn't easy,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.440
and neither is finding a way to fit all that
effects work into the budget.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.500
Sure, you can keep him in Bruce Banner mode
for a lot of the time, but then viewers will

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.799
just bide their time waiting for him to Hulk
out.

00:03:54.799 --> 00:04:01.000
If the budget differences aren't obvious,
2008's The Incredible Hulk cost $10 million

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.780
more to make than Iron Man, and Hulk didn't
even need to have any fancy armor fabricated.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:13.629
The Avengers earned an Academy Award nomination
in the "Visual Effects" category, so the expectations

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:19.410
to make a Hulk that doesn't look like a "ticked-off
Shrek", as one reviewer called Ang Lee's Hulk,

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.970
are very high.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.080
And very costly.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.860
Hulk is better on a team

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.061
Though the Hulk left the Avengers after his
first adventure with them in the comics, he

00:04:29.061 --> 00:04:32.430
just works better when he's surrounded by
other characters.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.590
That's not to say that the story of Bruce
Banner isn't compelling, but we've already

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.010
had two attempts to dig into it with minimal
success.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:44.530
There's also the fact that the Hulk is basically
a force of nature, and it's hard to give him

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:50.350
a bad guy that he can't just smash into bits…
or rip in half.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:56.100
But in the Avengers films, ultra-powerful
bad guys are standard, so the heroic Hulk

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.790
can still seem like an underdog when the battle
royale kicks off.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.570
Hulk's inner struggle

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:08.370
The Hulk is a great character, but when you
translate him from page to screen, it's not

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:11.590
easy to tell a compelling Hulk story.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:12.590
Why?

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:16.950
His biggest struggle is within himself, and
that's not an easy thing to showcase in a

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:21.500
medium that typically relies on explosions
instead of thought bubbles.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:26.210
Some of the best Hulk stories are character
studies, and that doesn't usually fit into

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.670
a successful superhero film.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:35.960
We can't have sad, walking-away piano music
all of the time…

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:39.720
...but it's a huge part of the character.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.980
Distribution rights

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.000
In addition to all the reasons we've already
listed, there's also been a lot of confusion

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.970
surrounding the film rights to a Hulk sequel.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.000
According to a Collider interview with Mark
Ruffalo,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.990
"Marvel doesn't really have the rights to
that yet.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:57.350
That's still Universal's property, so there's
that issue.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.530
That's a big impediment to moving forward
with that."

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:05.760
Ruffalo was kind of right, but the situation
is a bit more complicated.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:10.910
Universal apparently does have right of first
refusal to distribute a new Hulk film, which

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:15.850
could create some problems, but it's not a
big enough roadblock to keep Marvel from making

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:16.850
the movie.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:22.370
They famously cut a deal with Sony to share
Spider-Man, and the process is still pretty

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.840
complicated — but certainly far from impossible.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.970
Hulk gets pretty weird

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:34.920
Hulk dates back to the 1960s, during the glory
days of Stan Lee and Jack Kirby.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:40.070
If you've ever read any of Hulk's early appearances,
you know they can get a little strange, which

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:44.800
makes a truly authentic Hulk a little harder
to pull off on the big screen.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:50.850
"Captain America fighting Nazis" is a relatable
selling point for a film, but Hulk dressed

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:57.250
as a clown, juggling elephants, or being assaulted
in a YMCA shower might end up being a bit

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.940
more complicated for the multiplex.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.200
Even later stories like the fan-favorite World
War Hulk, which is reportedly being incorporated

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.970
to some extent into Thor: Ragnarok, and the
time-hopping Indestructible Hulk: Agent of

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:15.070
S.H.I.E.L.D. could be a big ask of typical
mainstream audiences.

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:16.650
Honestly, though….

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.110
This was weird enough.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:25.620
Thanks for watching!

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.690
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:32.430
And leave us a comment to let us know why
you think Hulk can't get his own movie...

